    <section id="manifesto">
      <div class="narrow lede">
        <p class="kicker">01 — Opening</p>
        <p>Remigration, on this page, means the lawful return of non-citizens who have no permanent right to remain: illegal entry, visa overstay, failed asylum, and criminal non-citizens after sentence. It is not a warrant against a person born a citizen. It is not a call to hurt anyone.</p>
<p>The reason the record is aimed at Muslim immigration, and not at immigration as a blur, is empirical. In Germany and Sweden the overrepresentation is measured. On the English Channel the arrivals are counted, and the nationalities rotate through Muslim-majority states. In those sending societies, majorities have told Pew they want religious law to outrank civil law. Japanese and Chinese nationals do not produce this picture. High-income work migrants do not produce it either.</p>
      </div>
    </section>
    <section class="band">
      <div class="narrow">
        <p class="kicker">Germany, police crime statistics, 2025</p>
        <blockquote>Non-German nationals were 35.5 percent of crime suspects. They were about 14.9 percent of the population.</blockquote>
        <p class="fine">The Bundeskriminalamt’s Polizeiliche Kriminalstatistik 2025 rounds the non-German suspect share to 35.5 percent (page 46). The table total behind that rounding, offense key 890000 excluding immigration-law offenses, is 677,951 non-German suspects out of 1,908,383 — 35.52 percent — against about 14.89 percent of the population at the end of 2025. These are suspects, not convictions. Dual nationals are counted as German, so foreign origin is understated, not inflated.</p>
      </div>
    </section>
    <section>
      <div class="wrap">
        <div class="narrow" style="margin:0">
          <p class="kicker">02</p>
          <h2>Four objections. None of them erase the multiple.</h2>
          <p class="deck">Every serious reading of these tables starts with the caveats. The caveats are real. They are not a solvent.</p>
        </div>
        <div class="claim-grid"><article class="claim">
        <p class="num">01</p>
        <h3>Age, poverty, and the postcode</h3>
        <p class="eyebrow">The objection</p>
        <p>Control for age, sex, income, and place, and the foreign-born gap in Sweden disappears.</p>
        <p class="eyebrow hot">The count</p>
        <p>It shrinks. It does not disappear. Brå report 2021:9, on suspicions from 2015 to 2018, puts the foreign-born at about 2.51 times the Swedish-born rate raw, about 2.25 after age and sex, and about 1.76 after income, education, and municipality. Swedish-born people with two foreign-born parents run 3.21, then 1.96, then 1.68. A gap that survives the controls is the finding.</p>
      </article>
<article class="claim">
        <p class="num">02</p>
        <h3>Only the recent illegal arrivals</h3>
        <p class="eyebrow">The objection</p>
        <p>The German figures are an artifact of asylum shelters and immigration offenses.</p>
        <p class="eyebrow hot">The count</p>
        <p>Immigration-law offenses are already out of the 35.52 percent. And the temporary-migrant slice is not the whole non-German share. Deutsche Welle’s account of the 2024 statistics: 8.8 percent of suspects were temporary migrants — asylum, illegal residence, or other temporary status — while 35.4 percent of suspects were non-German citizens. Most of the non-German share is the broader non-citizen population, not the newest prohibited arrival. People with no right to remain are still the part the law can return.</p>
      </article>
<article class="claim">
        <p class="num">03</p>
        <h3>Nationality is not Islam</h3>
        <p class="eyebrow">The objection</p>
        <p>A passport column is being laundered into a religious indictment.</p>
        <p class="eyebrow hot">The count</p>
        <p>Nationality is not a religion census, and this page will not pretend it is. The inference is narrower. The origin regions that dominate the elevated shares, and most of the small-boat nationalities, are Muslim-majority societies, and those societies poll for religious law. Albania is the stated exception in the British boat rotation. Japanese and Chinese nationals in Germany do not match the crime pattern. “Foreign” is the wrong noun.</p>
      </article>
<article class="claim">
        <p class="num">04</p>
        <h3>Suspects are not convictions</h3>
        <p class="eyebrow">The objection</p>
        <p>Until a court speaks, a suspect table is rumor.</p>
        <p class="eyebrow hot">The count</p>
        <p>A suspect is not a convict. Say so every time the number is used. Then say the rest. Among residents, the 2025 German suspect rate per 100,000 was 1,813.14 for Germans and 4,788.12 for non-Germans — about 2.6 times. That is a police-recording fact about who enters the crime statistic. It is not a conviction rate. It is also not nothing.</p>
      </article></div>
