    <section>
      <div class="narrow prose">
        <p class="kicker">Sweden and the Channel</p>
        <h2>The same shape, two other public ledgers.</h2>
        <p>Sweden’s National Council for Crime Prevention does not publish a Muslim census of offenders, and this page will not invent one. What Brå 2021:9 does publish is overrepresentation of the foreign-born in registered suspicions, and a second generation that stays elevated after the controls above. By region of birth the foreign-born are not one bloc: shares of suspicions run from about 3 percent to about 19 percent (page 53). Brå 2025:7 then splits the migrant stream the useful way. People from high-income countries, and people admitted to work or study, are at or under the Swedish-born rate. People from low-income and conflict-origin countries are higher.</p>
<p>On the offenses that dominate the politics, Sverigefakta’s reading of Brå 2021:9 figure 13b — age and sex adjusted, not the fully controlled model — puts foreign-born overrepresentation at about 3.6 times for lethal violence, 2.9 times for rape, and 3.5 times for robbery. The overall gap shrinks when income, education, and municipality are added. The offense multiples above are the age-and-sex figures. Use them at that strength and no further.</p>
<p>Britain’s small boats are a headcount, not a crime table. The House of Commons Library, for the year ending June 2026, records about 33,000 arrivals, about 72 percent of them adult men, accounting for about 88 percent of detected unauthorised arrivals. About 205,000 people have arrived this way since 2018. The peak was about 46,000 in 2022. 2025 was about 41,000. Nationalities rotate: Afghanistan, Iran, Syria, Eritrea, Sudan, Iraq, Albania. Five of those seven are Muslim-majority states. Albania is not. Eritrea is religiously mixed. Do not write “all of them.”</p>
<p>One English city already ran the experiment of refusing to describe a pattern. The Alexis Jay inquiry, published in 2014, found at least 1,400 children sexually exploited in Rotherham between 1997 and 2013. Officials looked away in part because they were afraid of being accused of racism. That is not a German suspect table. It is a warning about what a taboo costs.</p>
      </div>
    </section>
    <section class="band">
      <div class="narrow prose">
        <p class="kicker">04</p>
        <h2>Why the noun is Muslim immigration.</h2>
        <p>A pause aimed at “everyone foreign” would be a lie about the tables. Brå’s own later report says work migrants and migrants from rich countries are not the problem group. The German pattern is not the Japanese or Chinese pattern. The noun has to survive contact with those facts.</p>
<p>It does, if the noun is specific. The overrepresented origin regions, and the small-boat nationalities other than Albania, are overwhelmingly Muslim-majority. Pew asked those societies about the law they want, and the answers are not a Western separation of mosque and statute.</p>
<p>Keep the two Pew questions apart. In The World’s Muslims (2013), 99 percent of Afghan respondents and 84 percent of Pakistani respondents said they favored sharia as the official law of the land. That is a 2013 question about official religious law. It is not the 2025 question.</p>
<p>On January 28, 2025, Pew reported a different item: in Bangladesh, Indonesia, and Malaysia, adults more often say that when the Quran and the will of the people conflict, the Quran should weigh more. In Bangladesh that share was 77 percent. Do not add 99 to 77. Do not pretend the wording is the same. Do read both as evidence that large majorities in these societies want scripture to outrank the electorate.</p>
      </div>
    </section>
    <section>
      <div class="narrow prose">
        <h2>Private belief is not the cargo. The polity is.</h2>
        <p>A person may pray, fast, and keep a private table. That is not what an admissions policy imports. It imports the law the majority of the sending society says it wants, and it imports the offending pattern the receiving country’s own statistics already measure.</p>
<p>Asylum is temporary protection. It is not a population transfer and it does not ripen into a right to remain when the claim fails. The United States does not need a made-up border number to know this. Deportability of non-citizens is already 8 U.S.C. § 1227. Citizenship obtained by fraud can already be undone under 8 U.S.C. § 1451. Belief is not fraud. A lie on the application is.</p>
<p>The sister page, demandingremigration.com, turns the record into a statute: who leaves, whose visa is paused, and who is not touched. nozabiha.com and nozabihahalal.com are the food-jurisdiction half of the same refusal. islamophobe.me is the legal system. industrialterrorism.com is the other record.</p>
        <p><a href="https://demandingremigration.com/">Continue to demandingremigration.com</a></p>
      </div>
    </section>
    <section class="sand">
      <div class="narrow">
        <p class="kicker">The line in the sand</p>
        <h2>Muslim immigration from societies that want religious law over civil law is not compatible with the West, and the suspect tables are why the argument is no longer theoretical.</h2>
        <div class="prose"><p>It will not become compatible by changing the noun to “diversity,” or by calling a 2.6× suspect rate a misunderstanding. The controls have been run. The boats have been counted. The polls have been published.</p>
<p>Citizens are not the target. A faith practiced in private is not the target. The target is the continued import of a population whose origin societies reject equal civil law, into countries that already record the cost in their police statistics.</p>
<p>This page does not call for a riot, a vigilante, or a harm. It calls for the number to be said in public, with the caveats attached and the caveats not used as a gag.</p></div>
      </div>
    </section>
    <section id="act">
      <div class="wrap">
        <h2>What to do with a count.</h2>
        <div class="action-grid"><article class="action">
        <h3>Read the count first</h3>
        <p>A suspect share is not a conviction, and a nationality is not a mosque roll. Say both limits out loud, then look at the multiple. The multiple is still there.</p>
      </article>
<article class="action">
        <h3>Name the origin, not “foreign”</h3>
        <p>Japanese and Chinese nationals in Germany do not match this pattern. High-income and work migrants in Sweden do not match it either. The elevated shares track particular origin regions, most of them Muslim-majority.</p>
      </article>
<article class="action">
        <h3>Separate the boat from the passport</h3>
        <p>Small-boat arrivals are not a single faith. Albania is in the rotation and Albania is not Muslim-majority. Afghanistan, Iran, Syria, Iraq, and Sudan are. Do not flatten the list, and do not pretend the list is random.</p>
      </article>
<article class="action">
        <h3>Hand the record to the statute</h3>
        <p>This page is the count. The demand — who leaves, who never gets the visa, who is not touched — is the sister site. A count without a lawful next step is just a mood.</p>
      </article></div>
